(;GM[1]FF[4]CA[UTF-8]SZ[19]KM[0]RU[Japanese]EV[Ear-reddening game]DT[1846-09-11,14,15]PC[Edo]PB[Yasuda (Honinbo) Shusaku]BR[4d]PW[Inoue Gennan Inseki]WR[8d]RE[B+2]SO[Move list from Andries Brouwer's game archive, https://homepages.cwi.nl/~aeb/go/games/games/Shusaku/126.sgf, checked 2026-09-14 against Wikipedia, "Ear-reddening game" (moves 1–25 listed there match, date 1846-09-11, Shusaku Black, B+2). Comments are Go Master's own.]C[Gennan Inseki (White, 8 dan) against the 17-year-old Shusaku (Black, 4 dan), Edo, 1846. No komi: in Shusaku's day Black paid nothing for moving first. Watch how the first six moves take the four corners on 3-4 points, then the enclosures and approaches begin. Use the arrows or ← → to step through.]
;B[qd]C[R16, the 3-4 point. Shusaku's favourite first move; it looks at the corner and asks for one more stone to enclose it.];W[dc]C[D17, also a 3-4 point, in a second corner. The four corners are the biggest places on the board and are taken first.];B[pq]C[Q3: a third corner, a third 3-4 point.];W[oc]C[P17: White approaches Black's stone at R16 instead of taking the last corner — an approach (kakari) is worth about as much as an empty corner.];B[cp]C[C4: Black takes the last empty corner rather than answer. Four corners in six moves is the normal pace.];W[cf]C[C14: White encloses the top-left corner with a large knight's move from D17. An enclosure (shimari) makes the corner nearly territory.];B[ep]C[E4: Black's own enclosure from C4, a small knight's move. The two enclosures face each other down the left side.];W[qo]C[R5: White approaches Q3.];B[pe]C[Q15, the diagonal move that later took Shusaku's name: it answers the approach at P17 quietly and solidly. "I do not know a stronger move," he is supposed to have said of it.];W[np]C[O4: a second approach on the other side of Q3 — a pincer-like double approach, starting a fight. What follows to move 25 is the taisha, one of the longest and most complicated of all joseki; the joseki track will come back to it. For the opening, the lesson is over: corners, enclosures, approaches, in that order.]
;B[po];W[pp];B[op];W[qp];B[oq];W[oo];B[pn];W[qq];B[nq];W[on]
;B[pm];W[om];B[pl];W[mp];B[mq]C[The opening is finished on the right; the stones from 11 to 25 are the taisha. From here on, this is a middlegame — and later, at move 127, the ear-reddening move. The whole game is studied in the last track.];W[ol];B[pk];W[lq];B[lr];W[kr]
